      "worked": "Mary is correct. Bob’s derivative formula requires $\\sqrt{x^2-x^4}\\ne0$, so $x=0$ cannot be accepted as a zero-gradient point. In fact, $f(x)=|x|\\sqrt{1-x^2}$ has a corner at the origin: its left and right gradients are $-1$ and $1$. For the remaining candidates,\n$$\\begin{align*}x&=\\pm\\frac{1}{\\sqrt2}\\\\f\\!\\left(\\pm\\frac{1}{\\sqrt2}\\right)&=\\sqrt{\\frac12-\\frac14}=\\frac12.\\end{align*}$$\nHence the points are $\\left(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt2},\\frac12\\right)$ and $\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt2},\\frac12\\right)$.",
